# AST2050 BMC debug console (UART2 @ 0x1e784000), wired to the Pi's GPIO14/15
# hardware UART. Install on the ASUS bridge Pi as /etc/udev/rules.d/99-bmc-console.rules.
#
# Validated 2026-07-08: driving BMC UART2 over culvert P2A reads byte-perfect on this
# port at 1200 8N1 (the 2 jumpers were originally crossed — BMC-TXD was on GPIO14; now
# BMC-TXD -> Pi GPIO15/pin10, BMC-RXD -> Pi GPIO14/pin8, GND pin6).
#
# 2026-07-08: switched to the PL011 for reliable 115200. The Pi 4 mini-UART (ttyS0)
# has a core-clock-dependent baud that is only stable at 1200; the PL011 (ttyAMA0)
# has a fixed clock and is reliable at 115200. Applied on the Pi:
#   /boot/firmware/config.txt : + dtoverlay=disable-bt   (routes PL011 -> GPIO14/15,
#                                                          frees it from Bluetooth)
#   /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt : removed console=serial0,115200  (the Pi's own kernel
#                                console was sharing this UART and injecting noise)
# After a reboot, /dev/serial0 -> ttyAMA0 and this rule names it serial-bmc-console.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", KERNEL=="ttyAMA0", SYMLINK+="serial-bmc-console"
